Falsifies `paging::aarch64::index_is_always_in_bounds`.

The defect: the index field is taken as ten bits rather than nine. That is the width a 32-bit
x86 or an Sv32 table uses (1024 entries of four bytes to the page), and it is the wrong width
for every format in this tree, all three of which put 512 eight-byte entries in a 4 KiB frame.
An index of 512 or more is read and written past the end of `PageTable::entries`, which is the
out-of-bounds write the walk must never be able to perform.

Expected to fail: `assert!(Aarch64::index(va, level) < crate::ENTRIES)`, for any `va` whose
selected field has bit 9 set. The `aarch64` record is the one that widens the mask; `sv39`'s
drops it and `x86_64`'s moves the table size out from under it, so the three are not copies.

**The index math is shared and the proof is not.** `PageFormat::index` is one default method in
`lib.rs` and DECISIONS §19 (architectural parity) has each ISA state the property itself, so
one defect here is caught
three times over.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -478,7 +478,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
     /// [`LEVELS`](Self::LEVELS) so it is right for both a 4- and a 3-level walk.
     fn index(va: u64, level: usize) -> usize {
         let shift = 9 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
-        ((va >> shift) & 0x1ff) as usize
+        ((va >> shift) & 0x3ff) as usize
     }
 
     /// Does `va` lie in `half` for this format? The top bits (above [`SPLIT_SHIFT`](Self::SPLIT_SHIFT))
